  "interpretation": [
    "The rising send duration is primarily rising on-CPU time, not socket blocking or runnable starvation. Final-minute paired CPU is 94.23 percent of send wall.",
    "The sender remains one synchronous output pthread. With unchanged final-minute send cost, removing all between-send idle time cannot sustain 60 fps.",
    "The coarse thermal state rises from fair to serious and CPU work slows; this does not identify frequency, core choice, temperature, or establish thermal causality.",
    "The requested QoS remains default. Point-in-time priority fields do not identify effective QoS, donation, CPU core, or frequency.",
    "CPU timing covers classification, codec, protocol, socket and instrumentation work between hooks; JPEG-only CPU attribution is not measured."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Only send_thread_cpu_ms measures CPU time; it covers the whole sending thread between hooks, not just JPEG.",
    "send_wall_minus_thread_cpu_ms includes waiting/descheduling and small endpoint overhead; it does not distinguish their causes.",
    "Requested pthread QoS excludes overrides; raw Mach priority fields identify neither effective QoS nor CPU core or frequency.",
    "Nested stage durations overlap; do not add update/callback/preparation totals to their components.",
    "Source dirty count is the CARenderServer display counter, not the Safari animation sequence.",
    "Thermal state is categorical and sampled once per ten-second window, not a temperature measurement.",
